# id: M74-noop-reaches-a-state-machine-arm
# expect: killed
# covering-test: TestATermStartNoOpMatchesNoStateMachineArm
# package: ./store/
# power-covered-by: TestATermStartNoOpMatchesNoStateMachineArm -- a DIRECTED test on the entry raft actually produces. Becoming leader appends the no-op, the loopback scheduler lets it commit, and the two propositions are then counted on a real applied entry rather than hoped for across a sweep. It asserts its own non-vacuity first: no no-op applied means the two zeros are statements about an entry that does not exist
# power-refutation: this class named TestRaftExitCriteria, a 10,000-seed sweep costing 19 hours per invocation at the exit run's measured 6.9 s/seed. Seven mutants named it, so make mutants -- which is in the ci target -- asked for six days for that one covering test, and its baseline timed out and reported INVALID. A covering test built on a sweep stops covering when the workload moves and does it silently; this one is directed and cannot
# mutates: the apply switch's last arm drops its len(e.Data) > 0 guard, so a term-start no-op falls into the KV command path and is decoded as a command. D-A7-6's FIRST proposition -- no committed entry with empty Data ever reaches a state-machine arm -- negated. Ansh, ruling A: a guard whose stated purpose no longer covers everything resting on it is exactly how BUG-022 happened, so each of the two guards gets its own mutant per section 22.6b: they are independently removable and a single mutant would pass on half the protection
